# Multimeter Project Guidance

## Architecture

- `core/` is the platform-neutral TypeScript library for parsing, execution, network logic, reports, and conversion.
- `mmtview/` is the React and VS Code webview UI for editing and running `.mmt` files.
- `mmtcli/` contains the `testlight` CLI.
- Root `src/` contains the VS Code extension host code.
- `docs/` contains user-facing documentation. Use `https://mmt.dev` for the website and `https://test.mmt.dev` for the test server.

## Execution Rules

- `core/src/runner.ts` is the single source of truth for running `.mmt` files.
- Use `runner.runFile({ rawFile, filePath, inputs, envvar, fileLoader, jsRunner, logger })` instead of reimplementing parsing or execution in the extension, CLI, or webview.
- Keep `core/` free of VS Code, `fs`, `path`, browser APIs, and runtime-specific globals. Inject file loading, JS execution, logging, and network plumbing.
- Keep token matching and replacement logic for `e:`, `i:`, `r:`, and `c:` tokens in `core/src/variableReplacer.ts`.

## Change Strategy

- Implement behavior in `core/` first when it affects parsing, execution, or shared data models.
- Then wire behavior into `src/`, `mmtview/`, `mmtcli/`, and docs as needed.
- Update relevant data model types in `core/src/*Data.ts` before adding behavior that changes `.mmt` structure.
- Add or update focused tests in `core/src/*.test.ts` for pure logic changes.

## Style

- Use 2-space indentation and no tabs.
- Always use braces for control structures, including single-line `if`, `else`, loops, and switches.
- Prefer existing local helpers, UI patterns, and runner APIs over new abstractions.
- Keep changes scoped and avoid unrelated refactors.

## Validation

- Run `npm run compile --silent` from the repository root for the full build.
- Run `npm run test` for Jest tests.
- Use `vsce package` from the root to build the VS Code extension package.
